{"name":"Flint Smoke","hex":"#a8b2b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flint","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard, fine-grained quartz that fractures conchoidally and generates sparks when struck against a material such as steel, because tiny chips of the steel are heated to incandescence and burn in air.","A piece of flint, such as a gunflint, used to produce a spark by striking it with a firestriker."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish or decorate an object with flint."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flint"},{"term":"smoke","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible vapor/vapour, gases, and fine particles given off by burning or smoldering material.","A cigarette."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To inhale and exhale the smoke from a burning cigarette or cigar, burning material in a pipe, etc.","To inhale and exhale tobacco smoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoke"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flint","blocks":[{"text":"Flint, occasionally flintstone (SiO2), is a sedimentary cryptocrystalline form of the mineral quartz, categorized as the variety of chert that occurs in chalk or marly limestone. Historically, flint was widely used to make stone tools and start fires."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Miorcani_flint.jpg/330px-Miorcani_flint.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flint"},{"title":"Smoke","blocks":[{"text":"Smoke is an aerosol emitted when a material undergoes combustion or pyrolysis, together with the quantity of air that is entrained or otherwise mixed into the mass. It is commonly an unwanted by-product of fires, but may also be used for pest control (fumigation), communication, defensive and offensive capabilities in the military, cooking, or smoking. It is used in rituals where incense, sage, or resin is burned to produce a smell for spiritual or magical purposes. It can also be a flavoring agent and preservative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg/330px-Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoke"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Flint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg/500px-Flint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Flint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg/1280px-Flint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Flint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg/500px-Flint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A piece of flint 9–10 cm (3.5–3.9 in) long, weighing 171 grams","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlint%2C_9cm%2C_171gm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Silica_pseudomorph_after_halite_crystals_and_silicified_remains_of_algae_6.jpg/500px-Silica_pseudomorph_after_halite_crystals_and_silicified_remains_of_algae_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Silica_pseudomorph_after_halite_crystals_and_silicified_remains_of_algae_6.jpg/1280px-Silica_pseudomorph_after_halite_crystals_and_silicified_remains_of_algae_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Silica_pseudomorph_after_halite_crystals_and_silicified_remains_of_algae_6.jpg/500px-Silica_pseudomorph_after_halite_crystals_and_silicified_remains_of_algae_6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silicified remains of algae and silica pseudomo